## Implementation Phases

### Phase 1: Core Foundation (DONE)
Email/password auth with Argon2id hashing, JWT generation (access + refresh tokens), token validation/refresh/revocation, session tracking, password policy enforcement, account lockout, basic guards (@UseAuthGuard, @Public, @CurrentUser), TypeORM integration with PostgreSQL adapter.

### Phase 2: Verification System (DONE)
Email verification (code/link, 6-digit OTP, 1hr expiry), phone verification (SMS OTP, 5min expiry), ConsoleEmailProvider, NodemailerProvider, email templates, resend rate limiting.

### Phase 3: Social Login (TODO)
OAuth2 with Google, Apple, Facebook. Automatic account linking by email, bidirectional authentication (social→password, password→social), built-in provider attribute mappings, efficient database lookups with hasSocialAuth flag.

### Phase 4: Multi-Factor Authentication (TODO)
TOTP/authenticator apps with QR codes, SMS MFA, Passkey/WebAuthn, Email MFA, backup codes, trusted device memory, adaptive MFA triggers (new device/IP/country), enforcement modes (optional/required/adaptive).

### Phase 5: Advanced Session Management (TODO)
Device fingerprinting (IP/user-agent/screen/timezone), geolocation tracking (MaxMind/ipapi/ipinfo with country/city/ISP), session notifications (email/SMS/push on new device), suspicious activity detection, concurrent session policies.

### Phase 6: Security Features (TODO)
Rate limiting (memory/Redis storage, per-IP/user/endpoint), IP blocking (manual/automatic/geo-blocking), disposable email detection, comprehensive audit logging, session hijacking detection, brute force protection. Redis required for multi-server deployments.

### Phase 7: Advanced Token Features (TODO)
Token utilities (validate/decode/introspect), device-specific revocation, secret rotation mechanism, expired token cleanup jobs, detailed token metadata tracking.

### Phase 8: Database Adapters (TODO)
MySQL adapter with optimizations, SQLite adapter for testing, adapter test suite, migration compatibility across all adapters.

### Phase 9: Developer Experience (TODO)
CLI tool (migrations/user management), testing module with helpers, documentation site, example apps (REST/GraphQL), migration guides from Cognito/Auth0, troubleshooting guide.

---

## Security Requirements

**Authentication:** Strong password policies, MFA support, account lockout, token rotation, password breach detection (HaveIBeenPwned), credential enumeration prevention, timing attack prevention.

**Cryptography:** Argon2id password hashing (65536KB memory, 3 iterations, 4 threads), RS256/HS256 JWT signing, encryption at rest for sensitive data (TOTP secrets, OAuth tokens), secure cookie flags (httpOnly/Secure/SameSite), TLS for Redis.

**Attack Prevention:** XSS protection (httpOnly cookies, CSP), CSRF protection (SameSite cookies), SQL injection prevention (parameterized queries), rate limiting, IP blocking, session hijacking detection, token reuse detection, brute force protection.

**Compliance:** GDPR data protection (PII encryption/masking, right to erasure, data export, retention policies), CCPA compliance, audit logging (90d retention), security headers (HSTS, CSP, X-Frame-Options), OWASP Top 10 adherence.

**Multi-Server:** Redis required for rate limiting/account lockout/MFA state in ECS/Kubernetes/cluster deployments. Session revocation uses database automatically (works in all deployments). In-memory storage only for single-server/development.

---

## Token Management Strategy

**Storage Recommendations:**
- Web Apps (SPA): httpOnly cookies (most secure, XSS-proof, CSRF-proof with SameSite)
- Mobile Apps: Secure storage (iOS Keychain, Android EncryptedSharedPreferences) + header tokens
- Hybrid: Memory access token + httpOnly refresh cookie

**JWT Configuration:**
- Access Token: RS256 (prod multi-service) or HS256 (single app), 15min expiry
- Refresh Token: HS256, 30d expiry, automatic rotation with family tracking, reuse detection
- Token revocation via sessions table (isRevoked flag), no separate blacklist

**Security:** Token reuse detection (family pattern), refresh token rotation on every use, session hijacking detection (IP/user-agent validation), device fingerprinting, concurrent session limits.

---

## Performance & Metrics

**Performance Targets:** Login <200ms, token validation <50ms, session lookup <100ms, 10K+ concurrent users, indexed queries <100ms, token refresh <150ms.

**Success Metrics:** 1K+ GitHub stars in 6 months, 100+ weekly npm downloads, 10+ community contributors, zero critical security vulnerabilities, 90%+ test coverage, <5% bundle size overhead, production security audit passed.

---

## Phase 3 Design Refinements (Social Login)

**Optimized Table Structure:** nauth_users.hasSocialAuth flag prevents unnecessary joins for password-only users (80%+ of users). socialProviders array provides quick provider list without table join.

**No Token Storage:** Import user attributes once during OAuth (email, firstName, lastName), manage our own user directory, no subsequent provider API calls, simplified schema with fewer security concerns.

**Built-in Mappings:** Google (given_name→firstName), Apple (name.firstName→firstName), Facebook (first_name→firstName). No user configuration required.

**Account Linking:** Automatic by verified email (configurable per provider), supports password↔social and social↔social linking, multiple providers per user (Google + Apple + Facebook on single account), social-only users can add password later.

**Database:** nauth_social_accounts table (id, userId FK, provider, providerId, providerEmail, linkedAt, lastUsedAt, metadata). UNIQUE constraint on (provider, providerId). Indexes on userId and providerEmail.

---

